First, recall the structure:

(a) Scope: i) Has there been an assembly? ii) Did the applicant try to participate? iii) Was the assembly peaceful?

If any of these fails, no violation.

(b) Interference: Did some governmental act or omission interfere with the applicant's freedom of assembly? This includes arrests, dispersal, etc.

(c) Prescribed by law: Is the interference sufficiently foreseeable given the wording of the law? Is the provision that empowers government sufficiently accessible? Does the provision come with sufficient safeguards against abuse?

(d) Legitimate aim: Did the interference serve one of the legitimate aims listed in Art 11(2): national security, public safety, prevention of disorder, prevention of crime, protection of health, protection of morals, or protection of the rights and freedoms of others?

(e) Necessity in a democratic society: Did the government adequately balance the intensity of the intervention with the weight of the legitimate aim? Proportionality test.

